'\" t
.TH NINIT 8 2026-09-30 "ninit @PACKAGE_VERSION@"
.SH NAME
ninit \- init system driven by a compiled service dependency graph
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B init=@sbindir@/ninit
.RB [ ninit_graph=\c
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-
.IR path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ninit
runs as process 1.
it mounts the kernel filesystems, loads the dependency graph written by
.BR "ninitctl init" ,
starts each service when its prerequisites are ready, supervises the services, serves a control socket, and stops the system on request
.PP
services and the graph are described in
.BR ninit.d (5).
the graph is read once, at boot.
a graph rebuilt while the system runs takes effect at the next boot
.PP
started as any process other than 1,
.B ninit
exits with status 1
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I path
an argument beginning with
.B /
names the graph to load.
it takes precedence over
.BR ninit_graph .
the kernel passes to init every argument that follows
.B \-\-
on its command line
.SH BOOT
.B ninit
performs, in order:
.IP 1. 4
opens
.I /dev/null
on any of descriptors 0 to 2 that is closed, sets the umask to 022 and the working directory to
.I /
.IP 2. 4
blocks every signal except
.BR SIGSEGV ,
.BR SIGBUS ,
.B SIGILL
and
.BR SIGFPE ,
receives the signals in
.B SIGNALS
through a signalfd, and has ctrl-alt-del delivered as
.B SIGINT
.IP 3. 4
mounts each filesystem in the table below that is not already mounted, creating its mount point.
a failure is logged as a warning
.IP 4. 4
creates the symbolic links
.IR /dev/fd ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.I /dev/stdout
and
.IR /dev/stderr ,
and
.I /dev/core
if
.I /proc/kcore
exists
.IP 5. 4
creates
.IR /sys/fs/cgroup/ninit.services .
if
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
is not cgroup2 or the directory cannot be created, services are contained by process group only
.IP 6. 4
creates
.I /run/ninit
with mode 0700 and listens on
.I /run/ninit/control
with mode 0600
.IP 7. 4
sets its own
.I oom_score_adj
to \-1000
.IP 8. 4
directs its log to
.I /dev/console
.IP 9. 4
raises its own
.B RLIMIT_NOFILE
.IP 10. 4
reads
.I /etc/locale.conf
for the service environment
.IP 11. 4
prints the
.B PRETTY_NAME
of
.I /etc/os-release
or
.I /usr/lib/os-release
.IP 12. 4
loads and verifies the graph.
if that fails, it starts the emergency shell
.IP 13. 4
starts every service that has no prerequisites
.PP
.TS
tab(|);
lB lB lB
l l l.
mount point|type|options
/proc|proc|nosuid,noexec,nodev
/sys|sysfs|nosuid,noexec,nodev
/dev|devtmpfs|nosuid,mode=0755
/run|tmpfs|nosuid,nodev,mode=0755
/dev/pts|devpts|nosuid,noexec,mode=0620,gid=5,ptmxmode=0666
/dev/shm|tmpfs|nosuid,nodev,mode=1777
/sys/fs/cgroup|cgroup2|nosuid,noexec,nodev,nsdelegate
.TE
.PP
.B ninit
does not remount the root filesystem, mount the entries of
.IR fstab ,
enable swap, set the hostname, load modules or start udev.
these tasks are services
.SH STARTUP
startup is event driven.
when a service becomes ready, each dependent whose prerequisites are now all up is started immediately.
services are started, retried, restarted and failed as described in
.BR ninit.d (5)
.PP
while a service is starting, each 10 s interval without other activity produces a
.B WAIT
line for every service still starting.
startup ends when no service is starting and no restart is pending.
.B ninit
then prints the failed and skipped services and the number of services up
.PP
processes reparented to process 1 are reaped
.SH LOG
each log line has the form
.PP
.RS
.BI [ sss.mmm ]
.I tag
.B >
.I message
.RE
.PP
where the time is seconds since
.B ninit
started and
.I tag
is one of:
.TP
.B DONE
a service became ready
.TP
.B NOTE
progress, and a line of service output prefixed with the service name
.TP
.B WAIT
a service is still starting
.TP
.B WARN
a problem that was recovered from
.TP
.B FAIL
a failure
.PP
every line is printed on the console and appended to a 128 KiB ring in memory, which overwrites its oldest lines.
.B ninitctl log
reads the ring
.PP
when built with
.BR \-\-enable\-quiet ,
only
.B WARN
and
.B FAIL
lines are printed on the console.
the ring receives every line.
the console is written without blocking.
output the console does not accept within 100 ms is dropped and counted, and the count is printed once output resumes.
the ring retains dropped lines
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
.I /run/ninit/control
is a stream socket in the unix domain.
a client sends one request line,
.I verb
optionally followed by a space and a service name, at most 1023 bytes including the newline.
.B ninit
replies with zero or more data lines beginning with
.RB \(dq .\~ \(dq,
then one line beginning with
.RB \(dq +\~ \(dq
on success or
.RB \(dq \-\~ \(dq
on failure, and closes the connection
.PP
the verbs are
.BR status ,
.BR log ,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR restart ,
.B resume
and
.BR reload ,
with the meanings given in
.BR ninitctl (8).
.B reload
is always refused
.PP
the request
.B "log watch"
is answered with the ring, then with each line as it is logged, and has no final line.
a watcher the ring overtakes receives one
.B WARN
line,
.BI "log: skipped " n " bytes" ,
in place of the lines it lost.
at most 4 connections watch at once
.PP
an operation belongs to the service, not to the connection.
a client that disconnects before the final line is dropped and its operation continues.
at most 8 connections are served at once.
the socket is not served once shutdown has begun
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM
reboot
.TP
.B SIGINT
reboot.
the kernel sends it on ctrl-alt-del
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
halt
.TP
.B SIGUSR2
power off
.TP
.B SIGCHLD
reap children
.PP
once shutdown has begun, further shutdown signals are ignored
.SH SHUTDOWN
.IP 1. 4
the services are stopped in reverse dependency order.
a service is sent
.B SIGTERM
when every service that depends on it has stopped, and
.B SIGKILL
when its
.B stop-timeout
expires.
independent services stop in parallel.
this step ends after 30 s
.IP 2. 4
every process is sent
.BR SIGTERM .
.B ninit
waits up to 5 s for all of them to exit
.IP 3. 4
the remaining processes are sent
.BR SIGKILL .
.B ninit
waits up to 2 s
.IP 4. 4
.B hwclock \-\-systohc \-\-utc
is run, with
.B \-\-localtime
in place of
.B \-\-utc
if the third line of
.I /etc/adjtime
is
.BR LOCAL ,
and killed after 5 s
.IP 5. 4
every swap area in
.I /proc/swaps
is disabled
.IP 6. 4
the control socket is removed
.IP 7. 4
the filesystems are synced and remounted read-only, most recently mounted first, in up to 3 passes.
a filesystem that remains writable is unmounted, and failing that, detached.
.I /
is remounted read-only last
.IP 8. 4
.BR reboot (2)
is called with the requested action
.PP
no service is restarted and the emergency shell is not respawned during shutdown
.SH EMERGENCY SHELL
the emergency shell is started when the graph is missing, unreadable, of another version or fails verification, when a service with
.B onfail: shell
exhausts its start attempts, and when memory runs out while it allocates its tables at boot.
.B ninit
continues to run, services unaffected by the failure continue to start, and
.BR ninitctl (8)
can be used from the shell
.PP
the console is first reset: a virtual terminal in graphics mode is returned to text mode, a raw keyboard to unicode, and the terminal attributes to sane values.
the shell runs on
.I /dev/console
in a new session with the console as its controlling terminal and the environment
.BR PATH ,
.B HOME=/
and
.B TERM=linux
on a virtual terminal or
.B TERM=vt220
otherwise.
the first of the following that can be executed is run:
.IP 1. 4
.I /sbin/sulogin
or
.IR /usr/sbin/sulogin ,
if built with
.B \-\-with\-sulogin
and the root entry of
.I /etc/shadow
holds a hash that is not empty and does not begin with
.B !
or
.B *
.IP 2. 4
the busybox given to
.BR \-\-with\-busybox ,
as
.B \-sh
.IP 3. 4
.IR /bin/sh ,
as
.B \-sh
.PP
the shell is started again when it exits.
.B ninit
stops restarting it after 5 consecutive exits within 1 s of starting, 2 consecutive failures to execute any shell, or 5 consecutive failures to start it
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ninit_graph
path of the graph to load, if it begins with
.BR / .
the kernel passes a
.IB name = value
parameter it does not recognise to init as an environment variable.
the default is
.I @ninit_svcdir@/depgraph
.PP
the environment of
.B ninit
is not passed to services
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B ninit
exits with status 1 when it is not process 1.
as process 1 it does not exit
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @ninit_svcdir@/depgraph
the compiled graph
.TP
.I /etc/locale.conf
locale variables for services
.TP
.I /etc/adjtime
hardware clock mode,
.B UTC
or
.B LOCAL
on its third line, read at shutdown
.TP
.I /run/ninit/control
control socket
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup/ninit.services/svc\-N
cgroup of the service with index
.I N
.TP
.I /etc/shadow
consulted before the emergency shell when built with
.B \-\-with\-sulogin
.SH NOTES
without
.BR \-\-with\-sulogin ,
the emergency shell is an unauthenticated root shell.
where the console is reachable remotely, as over a serial line or a BMC, access to the console is equivalent to root access.
with
.BR \-\-with\-sulogin ,
a root account without a usable hash, or a system on which
.B sulogin
cannot be executed, still receives an unauthenticated shell
.PP
a hardware clock that keeps local time needs
.B LOCAL
on the third line of
.I /etc/adjtime
.PP
.B ninit
must be started through a path whose last component is
.BR ninit .
.BR ninit\-shutdown (8)
identifies it by
.I /proc/1/comm
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ninitctl (8),
.BR ninit\-shutdown (8),
.BR ninit.d (5),
.BR bootparam (7),
.BR cgroups (7),
.BR sulogin (8)
